##############################################################################
# .trivyignore — Trivy vulnerability ignore list
#
# Used by the `security-scan` job in .github/workflows/deploy-aws.yml.
# Trivy scans the built Docker image for OS-level and application CVEs.
#
# Format (one per line):
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN  # Severity | Reason | Expiry (if known)
#
# Review this file quarterly. Remove entries when upstream patches ship.
# ALL ignores MUST have a documented rationale comment.
#
# Docs: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/latest/docs/configuration/filtering/
##############################################################################

# ── node:20-alpine OS-level CVEs (musl libc / Alpine) ────────────────────────

# CVE-2023-4806   # MEDIUM | glibc heap-use-after-free in getaddrinfo — Alpine
#                 # uses musl libc, not glibc; this CVE does not apply.
#                 # Upstream: https://security.alpinelinux.org/
# CVE-2023-4806

# CVE-2023-5156   # MEDIUM | glibc getaddrinfo — same as above; musl-only base.
# CVE-2023-5156

# ── npm package CVEs (accepted after risk assessment) ────────────────────────
# Add entries here only after:
#   1. Confirming the vulnerable code path is not reachable in production
#   2. Creating a tracking issue referencing the CVE
#   3. Getting security team sign-off
#
# Template:
# CVE-YYYY-NNNNN  # SEVERITY | Package | Reason | Tracking issue #

# ── Instructions for adding new ignores ──────────────────────────────────────
# When Trivy flags a CVE that you wish to accept:
#   1. Research the CVE on NVD (https://nvd.nist.gov/)
#   2. Confirm the affected code path is not exercised by this application
#   3. Add the CVE ID below with mandatory rationale comment
#   4. Open a tracking issue to revisit when a patch is available
#   5. Set an expiry comment (e.g. "# Re-evaluate after Alpine 3.22 ships")
